Revision history for Perl extension Run.

0.01  Thu Dec 18 14:50:57 1997
	- original version; created by h2xs 1.18

0.02:	Initial release (chain, or, and, pipe, env, redir).
	Redir very fragile in complicated situations (tests 10-13 may fail
	depending on what fd were used when started).

0.03:	fdopen() is in fact ">&=FOO", thus close of created filehandle
	invalidates FOO.  Changed to open().

	Some tests still do not work right.

0.04:	Redirections rewritten.  The flakiness had these causes:
	- process_close_in_kid() returned false for an empty list, so
	  redirecting fd N failed silently whenever N was the lowest free fd;
	- fd numbers were created by dup()ing up to N and never closed, so
	  each run changed which fds the next run found free;
	- a saved copy of one target could land on a later target, and the
	  guard against it compared a filehandle with fd numbers;
	- restoring with $^F back at 2 set close-on-exec on restored fds and
	  could move them; with $^F raised, leaked fds lost it instead
	  (hence "redir.t no. 13 is fragile with this");
	- the original F_GETFD result "0 but true" was passed to F_SETFD as a
	  string, i.e. as a pointer;
	- hash order decided the order of redirections at one level;
	- ">&=N" filenames were opened before outer redirections were applied;
	- "Bad file number" was matched in $! (Linux says "Bad file descriptor").
	Now: applied in the kid after fork; with $Run::Wraparound, applied in
	the parent around the spawn and restored exactly.  Ordered lists,
	">&N" and ">&-".  New test t/fdtable.t.

0.05:	new_pipeline: "a | b | c".  Elements other than simple commands
	(and, or, chain, pipeline) run in this process, which keeps the
	write end open until their last command has ended, as needed for
	"{ a || b } | c"; with fork, a second such element runs in a forked
	copy.
	Fixes:
	- new_system/new_spawn with one element ran it as a shell string;
	  now always "exec { PROG } LIST".  The exported spawn() is
	  unchanged;
	- {filename => NAME, mode => OP} opens NAME literally with sh's
	  < > >| >> <> (2-argument open misread "-", "&1", leading blanks,
	  and had no "<>");
	- $data->{result} was set only on failure and never reset; now the
	  $? of every system, 0 included, or -1 when nothing started (also
	  when a new_redir target cannot be opened);
	- new_chain failed if any command failed; now the last one decides;
	- new_readpipe and new_readpipe_split never reaped their kid (its
	  pid no longer appears in the caller's $data->{pids});
	- an exec failure was reported twice (also as a warning under -w);
	  messages no longer show a stale $!.
	Not tested: the list form "system { PROG } 1, LIST" on OS/2, Win32
	and VMS.
	New tests t/fixes.t and t/pipeline.t.
